\begin{abstract}
The tree of primitive Pythagorean triples is generated by three integer matrices
$M_1, M_2, M_3 \in \mathrm{O}(2,1;\ZZ)$ acting on column vectors $(a,b,c)^{\mathsf T}$ with
$a^2+b^2=c^2$. We determine the exact spectral data of these generators and the exact
behaviour of their reductions modulo an integer, and we show that the resulting
\emph{modular resonance frequencies} separate the prime factors of a semiprime.

Two of the generators, $M_1$ and $M_3$, are unipotent with characteristic polynomial
$(X-1)^3$ and nilpotency index exactly $3$; their powers admit closed polynomial forms,
from which we derive that their multiplicative order modulo any odd $m$ is exactly $m$,
and a \emph{factoring barrier}: every greatest common divisor extracted from a unipotent
branch divides $\gcd(k^2,m)$, so the branch carries no factoring information at all.

The middle generator $M_2$ is hyperbolic, with characteristic polynomial
$(X+1)(X^2-6X+1)$ and eigenvalues $-1$ and $3 \pm 2\sqrt2 = (1\pm\sqrt2)^{\pm2}$, the
square of the silver ratio and a norm-one unit of $\ZZ[\sqrt2]$. We prove, by an explicit
matrix Frobenius computation, that modulo an odd prime $p$ the order of $M_2$ divides
$p-1$ when $p \equiv \pm1 \pmod 8$ and divides $p+1$ when $p\equiv\pm3\pmod 8$, and always
divides $p^2-1$; exactly, $\ord_p(M_2) = \lcmop(2,\ord_p(U))$ where
$U = \left(\begin{smallmatrix}3&2\\4&3\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ is the hyperbolic block.
We prove further that $\lambda$ is an eigenvalue of $M_2$ over $\FF_p$ if and only if
$(\lambda+1)(\lambda^2-6\lambda+1)=0$, that the quadratic factor splits over $\FF_p$ if
and only if $2$ is a quadratic residue modulo $p$, and hence that the split/inert
dichotomy of the spectrum is literally the $p-1$ versus $p+1$ frequency dichotomy.

Combining these we obtain a provably correct factoring criterion: if an exponent $k$ is a
resonance of $p$ but not of $q$, then some entry $x$ of the integer matrix $M_2^{k}-I$
satisfies $\gcd(x,pq)=p$ exactly. We also prove the converse barrier: if $k$ is a
resonance of the whole modulus, every attainable gcd equals $N$. Finally we extract the
\emph{Berggren--Lucas trace sequence} $t(k)=\tr(M_2^k)$, obeying
$t(k+3)=5t(k+2)+5t(k+1)-t(k)$ with $t(0),\dots,t(3)=3,5,35,197$, and prove the
Fermat-type congruence $t(p)\equiv 5 \pmod p$ for every odd prime $p$, yielding a
linear-time compositeness test.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Pythagorean triples, Berggren tree, Lorentz group
$\mathrm{O}(2,1)$, silver ratio, quadratic reciprocity, Frobenius endomorphism, Lucas
sequences, integer factorization, Pollard $p-1$, Williams $p+1$.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The tree of right triangles}

A \emph{primitive Pythagorean triple} is a triple of positive integers $(a,b,c)$ with
$a^2+b^2=c^2$ and $\gcd(a,b,c)=1$. The set of all such triples forms an infinite ternary
tree rooted at $(3,4,5)$, whose edges are given by three fixed integer matrices. In the
normalization used throughout this paper these are
\[
M_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & -2 & 2 \\ 2 & -1 & 2 \\ 2 & -2 & 3\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
M_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 & 2 \\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\ 2 & 2 & 3\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
M_3 = \begin{pmatrix} -1 & 2 & 2 \\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\ -2 & 2 & 3\end{pmatrix},
\]
acting on column vectors. Applied to $(3,4,5)$ they yield $(5,12,13)$, $(21,20,29)$ and
$(15,8,17)$ respectively; iterating produces every primitive triple exactly once.

The structural reason for the tree's existence is that these matrices are integral
isometries of the Lorentz form
\[
Q(a,b,c) = a^2 + b^2 - c^2, \qquad Q = \diag(1,1,-1),
\]
so that Pythagorean triples are exactly the integral null vectors of a $(2{+}1)$-dimensional
Minkowski space and the tree is an orbit of a discrete group of Lorentz transformations.

\subsection{What this paper does}

We study the reductions modulo $N$ of the three generators and ask: after how many steps
does the Berggren dynamics return to the identity? We call this exponent the
\emph{resonant frequency} of the generator at the modulus. The results below determine it
completely, tie it to the eigenvalue structure of $M_2$ over $\FF_p$, and convert the
resulting arithmetic into a factoring criterion for semiprimes $N=pq$ and a compositeness
test. The three main conceptual findings are:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Only the hyperbolic branch carries arithmetic information: the unipotent generators
have order exactly the modulus and provably yield no factoring advantage.
\item The resonant frequency of the hyperbolic branch is $p-1$ or $p+1$ according to
$p \bmod 8$, and this dichotomy is exactly the dichotomy of whether the eigenvalues of
$M_2$ are visible in $\FF_p$ or hidden in $\FF_{p^2}$.
\item Frequency misalignment at the two primes of $N=pq$ is necessary and sufficient for
the resonance test to split $N$.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Relation to known factoring methods}

The factoring criterion obtained here belongs to the family of Pollard's $p-1$ and
Williams' $p+1$ methods, and, like them, it runs quickly precisely when $p-1$ or $p+1$ is
smooth. It is not a break of RSA, and primes generated for cryptographic use are chosen so
that neither $p\pm1$ is smooth. What is distinctive is (i) the provenance --- the criterion
is derived from the symmetry group of Pythagorean triples rather than postulated --- and
(ii) that a single fixed matrix realises both the $p-1$ and the $p+1$ mechanism
simultaneously, with the residue class of $p$ modulo $8$ automatically selecting the
operative one.

\section{Notation and preliminaries}

Throughout, $R$ is a commutative ring, $I$ is the identity matrix of the appropriate size,
and $\FF_p = \ZZ/p\ZZ$ for a prime $p$. For an integer matrix $A$ and $m \in \NN$ we write
$A \bmod m$ for the entrywise reduction; reduction is a ring homomorphism, so
$(A \bmod m)^k = A^k \bmod m$.

\begin{lemma}[Congruence criterion]\label{lem:cong}
For any $N \in \NN$ and any integer matrix $A$,
\[
A \equiv I \pmod N \iff N \mid (A - I)_{ij} \text{ for all } i,j.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Reduction mod $N$ is entrywise, and an element of $\ZZ/N\ZZ$ is zero exactly when the
integer representing it is divisible by $N$.
\end{proof}

We use the second supplement to quadratic reciprocity: for an odd prime $p$, the element
$2$ is a square in $\FF_p$ if and only if $p \equiv \pm 1 \pmod 8$; and Euler's criterion,
$a^{(p-1)/2} = \pm1$ in $\FF_p$ according to whether $a$ is a square.

\section{Exact spectral data of the generators}

\begin{proposition}
$\det M_1 = \det M_3 = 1$, $\det M_2 = -1$; $\tr M_1 = \tr M_3 = 3$, $\tr M_2 = 5$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Spectral classification]\label{thm:charpoly}
\[
\chi_{M_1}(X) = \chi_{M_3}(X) = (X-1)^3, \qquad
\chi_{M_2}(X) = (X+1)(X^2-6X+1).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Direct expansion of $\det(XI - M_i)$ for $3\times3$ matrices, followed by factorisation.
For $M_1$ one obtains $X^3-3X^2+3X-1$; for $M_2$ one obtains $X^3-5X^2-5X+1$, which has
the root $X=-1$ and quotient $X^2-6X+1$.
\end{proof}

Thus $M_1$ and $M_3$ are \emph{unipotent} and $M_2$ is \emph{hyperbolic}.

\begin{proposition}[Exact nilpotency index]
$(M_1-I)^3 = (M_3-I)^3 = 0$, while $(M_1-I)^2 \neq 0$ and $(M_3-I)^2\neq 0$. Hence the
nilpotency index of $M_i - I$ is exactly $3$ for $i \in \{1,3\}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Silver-ratio spectrum]\label{thm:silver}
The roots of $X^2-6X+1$ are $3 \pm 2\sqrt2$. In $\ZZ[\sqrt2]$,
\[
3 + 2\sqrt2 = (1+\sqrt2)^2, \qquad \mathrm{N}(3+2\sqrt2) = 3^2 - 2\cdot 2^2 = 1,
\qquad (3+2\sqrt2)(3-2\sqrt2)=1 .
\]
Thus the two hyperbolic eigenvalues of $M_2$ are mutually inverse units of norm $1$,
namely the square of the fundamental unit (the \emph{silver ratio}) $1+\sqrt2$ of
$\ZZ[\sqrt2]$.
\end{theorem}

Numerically $3+2\sqrt2 \approx 5.8284$: iterating $M_2$ multiplies the hypotenuse
asymptotically by this factor at each step, which is why the branch
$(3,4,5)\to(21,20,29)\to(119,120,169)\to\cdots$ grows geometrically.

\begin{proposition}[Minimal polynomial and eigenline]
$(M_2+I)(M_2^2-6M_2+I)=0$, and neither factor vanishes; hence $X+1$ and $X^2-6X+1$ are
precisely the irreducible constituents of the minimal polynomial of $M_2$. The vector
$(1,-1,0)^{\mathsf T}$ spans the $(-1)$-eigenline. Moreover $M_2^{\mathsf T}=M_2$, so
$M_2$ is diagonalizable over $\RR$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Integral isometries]\label{thm:lorentz}
Over every commutative ring $R$,
\[
M_i^{\mathsf T}\,\diag(1,1,-1)\,M_i = \diag(1,1,-1), \qquad i = 1,2,3 .
\]
Consequently, writing $\Phi(v) = v_0^2+v_1^2-v_2^2$, one has $\Phi(M_i v)=\Phi(v)$ for all
$v\in R^3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A finite verification of bilinear identities; since the statement is a polynomial identity
with integer coefficients it holds over $\ZZ$ and hence over every commutative ring by base
change.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Triples to triples, over $\ZZ$ and mod $N$]\label{cor:triples}
If $a^2+b^2=c^2$ over $\ZZ$ then the image $M_i(a,b,c)^{\mathsf T}$ again satisfies the
Pythagorean relation; the same holds over $\ZZ/N\ZZ$, so the Berggren dynamics preserves
the conic $a^2+b^2=c^2$ in $(\ZZ/N\ZZ)^3$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The unipotent branches: closed forms, exact order, and a factoring barrier}

\begin{theorem}[Unipotent closed forms]\label{thm:uniclosed}
For all $k \ge 0$,
\[
M_1^{k} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & -2k & 2k \\ 2k & 1-2k^2 & 2k^2 \\ 2k & -2k^2 & 1+2k^2\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
M_3^{k} = \begin{pmatrix} 1-2k^2 & 2k & 2k^2 \\ -2k & 1 & 2k \\ -2k^2 & 2k & 1+2k^2\end{pmatrix}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $k$. Conceptually, $M_i = I + \eta$ with $\eta^3=0$ gives
$M_i^k = I + k\eta + \binom{k}{2}\eta^2$, a matrix polynomial of degree $2$ in $k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact unipotent resonance]\label{thm:unires}
Let $m$ be odd. Then for every $k\ge0$,
\[
M_1^{k}\equiv I \pmod m \iff m \mid k ,
\]
and likewise for $M_3$. Consequently $\ord_m(M_1) = \ord_m(M_3) = m$ for odd $m>0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) The $(0,1)$ entry of $M_1^k-I$ is $-2k$; the congruence forces $m\mid 2k$,
and $m$ odd gives $m\mid k$. ($\Leftarrow$) If $m\mid k$ then $m$ divides $2k$ and $2k^2$,
so by Theorem~\ref{thm:uniclosed} the reduction is the identity. The order statement
follows because the resonance set is exactly $m\ZZ$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For a semiprime $N=pq$ with $p,q$ odd, $\ord_N(M_1) = pq$: the two prime frequencies are
not separated by the unipotent flow.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}[Entry classification]\label{lem:entries}
Every entry of $M_1^{k}-I$ is one of $0$, $\pm2k$, $\pm2k^2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Unipotent factoring barrier]\label{thm:barrier}
Let $N$ be odd and $k\ge0$. For every entry $x \neq 0$ of $M_1^{k}-I$,
\[
\gcd(x,N) \ \big|\ \gcd(k^2,N) .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Put $g = \gcd(x,N)$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:entries}, $g \mid 2k^2$; since $g\mid N$ and $N$ is
odd, $g$ is odd and coprime to $2$, so $g \mid k^2$. Combined with $g\mid N$ this gives
$g \mid \gcd(k^2,N)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No factoring advantage]\label{cor:noadv}
If a prime $r$ divides $\gcd(x,N)$ for some nonzero entry $x$ of $M_1^{k}-I$ with $N$ odd,
then $r\mid k$ and $r\mid N$. The unipotent branch can only reveal primes already present
in the exponent.
\end{corollary}

Resonance requires oscillation; a unipotent (polynomially drifting) flow has no phase to be
in or out of.

\section{Hyperbolic resonance: the frequencies $p \mp 1$}

\subsection{Block normal form}

Let
\[
W = \begin{pmatrix}1&0&1\\1&0&-1\\0&1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
U = \begin{pmatrix}3&2\\4&3\end{pmatrix},\qquad
V = \begin{pmatrix}3&2&0\\4&3&0\\0&0&-1\end{pmatrix} .
\]
The columns of $W$ are $(1,1,0)$ and $(0,0,1)$, spanning the plane on which $M_2$ acts
hyperbolically, and the $(-1)$-eigenvector $(1,-1,0)$.

\begin{theorem}[Integral block diagonalisation]\label{thm:block}
Over every commutative ring, $M_2 W = W V$, hence $M_2^{k}W = W V^{k}$ for all $k\ge0$.
Moreover $\det W = 2$, $\det U = 1$, and
$V^{k} = \left(\begin{smallmatrix} U^{k} & 0 \\ 0 & (-1)^k\end{smallmatrix}\right)$.
\end{theorem}

Since $\det W = 2$, the matrix $W$ is invertible over $\FF_p$ for every odd prime $p$, so
over $\FF_p$ the conjugation is a genuine change of basis.

\subsection{The block $U$ is the number $3+2\sqrt2$}

Write $S = \left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&2\\4&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:US}
$U = 3I + S$ and $S^2 = 8I$. Moreover $U(6I-U) = I$, so $U^{-1} = 6I - U$ and $U$ is a
unit of determinant $1$.
\end{lemma}

Thus $\ZZ[S]$ is a faithful matrix model of $\ZZ[2\sqrt2]$, with $S$ playing the role of
$2\sqrt2$ and $U$ the role of $3+2\sqrt2$.

\subsection{The Frobenius computation}

\begin{theorem}[Matrix Frobenius]\label{thm:frob}
Let $p$ be an odd prime. Over $\FF_p$,
\[
U^{p} = 3I + 8^{(p-1)/2}\, S .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $3I$ is central, $(3I+S)^p = (3I)^p + S^p$ in characteristic $p$; then
$(3I)^p = 3I$ by Fermat, and, writing $p = 2\cdot\frac{p-1}{2}+1$,
$S^p = (S^2)^{(p-1)/2}S = 8^{(p-1)/2}S$.
\end{proof}

Because $8=2^3$ and $\bigl(2^{(p-1)/2}\bigr)^2=1$, we have
$8^{(p-1)/2} = 2^{(p-1)/2} \in\{+1,-1\}$: the Legendre symbol of $2$.

\begin{theorem}[Block resonance]\label{thm:blockres}
Let $p$ be an odd prime.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $2^{(p-1)/2}=1$ in $\FF_p$ then $U^{p}=U$, hence $U^{p-1}=I$.
\item If $2^{(p-1)/2}=-1$ in $\FF_p$ then $U^{p}=6I-U=U^{-1}$, hence $U^{p+1}=I$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Case 1 is immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:frob} and $U=3I+S$; multiply by
$U^{-1}=6I-U$. Case 2: $8^{(p-1)/2}=-1$ gives $U^p = 3I-S = 6I-U$, and $U(6I-U)=I$ by
Lemma~\ref{lem:US}.
\end{proof}

Conceptually: in case 1 the eigenvalues $3\pm2\sqrt2$ lie in $\FF_p^\times$, of order
$p-1$; in case 2 they lie in $\FF_{p^2}$ and are Frobenius-conjugate, hence lie on the
norm-one torus of order $p+1$.

\subsection{Transport back to $M_2$}

\begin{lemma}[Transport]\label{lem:transport}
Let $p$ be an odd prime and suppose $U^{k}=I$ over $\FF_p$ with $k$ even. Then
$M_2^{k}\equiv I \pmod p$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$V^k = \diag(U^k,(-1)^k)=I$, so $M_2^kW = WV^k = W$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:block}; as
$\det W = 2 \neq 0$ in $\FF_p$, $W$ is invertible and $M_2^k=I$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Hyperbolic Resonance Theorem]\label{thm:hypres}
Let $p$ be an odd prime. Then
\[
p \equiv \pm 1 \!\!\pmod 8 \implies M_2^{p-1}\equiv I \!\!\pmod p,
\qquad
p \equiv \pm 3 \!\!\pmod 8 \implies M_2^{p+1}\equiv I \!\!\pmod p,
\]
and in all cases $M_2^{p^2-1}\equiv I \pmod p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Combine Euler's criterion with the second supplement to quadratic reciprocity to convert
$p \bmod 8$ into the value of $2^{(p-1)/2}$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:blockres} and
Lemma~\ref{lem:transport}. The uniform statement follows from $p^2-1=(p-1)(p+1)$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The exact resonance spectrum and the order formula}

\begin{theorem}[Exact resonance spectrum]\label{thm:exactres}
Let $p$ be an odd prime and $k\ge0$. Then
\[
M_2^{k}\equiv I \pmod p \iff \bigl(U^{k}=I \text{ in } \FF_p \text{ and } k \text{ even}\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Leftarrow$) is Lemma~\ref{lem:transport}. ($\Rightarrow$) Conjugating by the invertible
$W$ turns $M_2^k=I$ into $V^k=I$, i.e.\ $U^k=I$ and $(-1)^k=1$; the latter forces $k$ even
because $-1\ne1$ in $\FF_p$ for odd $p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Order formula]\label{cor:order}
For every odd prime $p$, $\ \ord_p(M_2) = \lcmop\bigl(2,\ \ord_p(U)\bigr)$.
\end{corollary}

The frequency bound $p\mp1$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:hypres} is attained exactly when $U$
generates the full cyclic group, a primitive-root condition. The bound is attained for
$p = 3,5,7,11,13,19,23,31,37,43,47,53,61,67,71$ and fails for $p=17$ (order $8$, not
$16$), $p=29$ (order $10$), $p=41$ (order $10$), $p=59$ (order $20$), $p=73$ (order $36$),
$p=79$ (order $26$).

\section{The eigenvalue distribution modulo $p$}

\begin{lemma}[Determinant in closed form]\label{lem:det}
Over any commutative ring and for every scalar $\lambda$,
$\det(M_2-\lambda I) = -(\lambda+1)(\lambda^2-6\lambda+1)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Eigenvalue criterion]\label{thm:eigcrit}
Let $p$ be a prime and $\lambda \in \FF_p$. There exists a nonzero $v\in\FF_p^3$ with
$M_2 v = \lambda v$ if and only if $(\lambda+1)(\lambda^2-6\lambda+1)=0$ in $\FF_p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Over a field, $(M_2-\lambda I)v=0$ has a nonzero solution iff $\det(M_2-\lambda I)=0$;
apply Lemma~\ref{lem:det}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
$\lambda=-1$ is an eigenvalue of $M_2$ modulo every prime, with eigenvector $(1,-1,0)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Splitting criterion]\label{thm:split}
Let $p$ be an odd prime. Then $X^2-6X+1$ has a root in $\FF_p$ if and only if $2$ is a
square in $\FF_p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) If $\lambda^2-6\lambda+1=0$ then $\bigl((\lambda-3)/2\bigr)^2 = 8/4 = 2$;
division by $2$ is legitimate as $p$ is odd. ($\Leftarrow$) If $2=s^2$ then
$\lambda=3+2s$ gives $\lambda^2-6\lambda+1 = 4s^2-8 = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Split case]\label{thm:splitcase}
Let $p$ be an odd prime with $p\equiv\pm1\pmod8$. Then $M_2$ has an eigenvalue
$\lambda\neq-1$ in $\FF_p$; both roots $3\pm2s$ (where $s^2=2$) exist in $\FF_p$, are
distinct, and differ from $-1$. The spectrum of $M_2$ over $\FF_p$ consists of three
eigenvalues.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Existence follows from Theorem~\ref{thm:split} and the second supplement. No root equals
$-1$: substituting $\lambda=-1$ into $\lambda^2-6\lambda+1$ gives $8=2^3$, and $2^3=0$ in a
field would force $2=0$, contradicting $p$ odd. Distinctness: the discriminant is
$32\neq0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Inert case]\label{thm:inert}
Let $p$ be an odd prime with $p\equiv\pm3\pmod8$. Then the only eigenvalue of $M_2$ in
$\FF_p$ is $-1$: any $\lambda$ with $(\lambda+1)(\lambda^2-6\lambda+1)=0$ satisfies
$\lambda=-1$. The hyperbolic pair is inert, living in $\FF_{p^2}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Spectrum implies frequency]\label{thm:spec2freq}
Let $p$ be an odd prime.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $M_2$ has an eigenvalue $\lambda\neq-1$ in $\FF_p$ (split spectrum), then
$M_2^{p-1}\equiv I\pmod p$.
\item If $-1$ is the only eigenvalue of $M_2$ in $\FF_p$ (inert spectrum), then
$M_2^{p+1}\equiv I\pmod p$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
In case 1, a root of the quadratic makes $2$ a square by Theorem~\ref{thm:split}, so
$2^{(p-1)/2}=1$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:blockres}(1) with Lemma~\ref{lem:transport} applies.
In case 2, if $2$ were a square the quadratic would have a root, which by hypothesis must
be $-1$, giving $2^3=0$ and hence $2=0$ --- impossible; so $2^{(p-1)/2}=-1$ and
Theorem~\ref{thm:blockres}(2) applies.
\end{proof}

Thus the resonant frequency is a spectral invariant: split spectrum $\Leftrightarrow$
frequency divides $p-1$; inert spectrum $\Leftrightarrow$ frequency divides $p+1$.

\begin{theorem}[Spectral misalignment at a semiprime]\label{thm:misalign}
Let $p,q$ be odd primes with $p\equiv\pm1\pmod 8$ and $q\equiv\pm3\pmod8$. Then
$M_2^{p-1}\equiv I \pmod p$ and $M_2^{q+1}\equiv I\pmod q$, and the hyperbolic spectrum of
$M_2$ is split at $p$ and inert at $q$. The two primes of $N=pq$ carry different spectral
types.
\end{theorem}

\section{Resonance and factorization}

\begin{theorem}[CRT decomposition of resonance]\label{thm:crt}
Let $m,n$ be coprime and $k\ge0$. Then $M_2^{k}\equiv I \pmod{mn}$ if and only if
$M_2^{k}\equiv I \pmod m$ and $M_2^{k}\equiv I \pmod n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:cong} the statement is entrywise divisibility; divisibility by $mn$
implies divisibility by each factor, and conversely coprime divisors of an integer have
divisible product.
\end{proof}

Hence factoring $N=pq$ is exactly the problem of separating two resonant frequencies inside
one modulus.

\begin{lemma}[Factor extraction]\label{lem:extract}
Let $p\ge1$ and let $q$ be prime. If $x\in\ZZ$ satisfies $p\mid x$ and $q\nmid x$, then
$\gcd(x,pq)=p$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $g=\gcd(x,pq)$. Then $p\mid g$; write $g=pt$ with $t\mid q$, so $t\in\{1,q\}$. If $t=q$
then $q\mid g\mid x$, contradicting $q\nmid x$; hence $g=p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Resonance extracts a prime factor]\label{thm:factor}
Let $N=pq$ with $p,q$ prime, and let $k$ satisfy
\[
M_2^{k}\equiv I \pmod p \qquad\text{but}\qquad M_2^{k}\not\equiv I \pmod q .
\]
Then there exist indices $i,j$ such that $x=(M_2^{k}-I)_{ij}$ satisfies $\gcd(x,N)=p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:cong} the first hypothesis says $p$ divides every entry of $M_2^k-I$; the
second says some entry $x$ is not divisible by $q$. Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:extract}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Canonical frequency]
If $p$ is an odd prime, $q$ a prime, and $M_2^{p^2-1}\not\equiv I\pmod q$, then $k=p^2-1$
splits $N=pq$ in the sense of Theorem~\ref{thm:factor}.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Sharp frequencies]
Let $p$ be an odd prime and $q$ prime. If $p\equiv\pm1\pmod8$ and
$M_2^{p-1}\not\equiv I\pmod q$, then $k=p-1$ splits $N=pq$; if $p\equiv\pm3\pmod8$ and
$M_2^{p+1}\not\equiv I\pmod q$, then $k=p+1$ splits $N=pq$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Alignment barrier]\label{thm:align}
If $M_2^{k}\equiv I \pmod N$, then $\gcd\bigl((M_2^{k}-I)_{ij},N\bigr)=N$ for all $i,j$.
Hence resonance misalignment is necessary for the method, not merely convenient.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Semiprime period bound]\label{thm:period}
Let $p\neq q$ be odd primes. Then modulo $N=pq$,
$M_2^{\lcmop(p^2-1,\,q^2-1)}\equiv I \pmod N$, a Carmichael-type bound.
\end{theorem}

\begin{example}[$N=15$]
$3\equiv3\pmod8$, so the frequency of $3$ is $4$; $5\equiv5\pmod8$ gives $5$ the frequency
$6$. Indeed $M_2^4\equiv I\pmod3$ while $M_2^4\not\equiv I\pmod5$, and
\[
M_2^{4}-I = \begin{pmatrix}288&288&408\\288&288&408\\408&408&576\end{pmatrix},
\qquad \gcd(288,15)=3 .
\]
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Textbook RSA modulus $N=3233=53\cdot61$]
$53\equiv5\pmod8$, so the frequency of $53$ divides $54$, while $61$ has frequency dividing
$62$. One verifies $M_2^{54}\equiv I\pmod{53}$ and $M_2^{54}\not\equiv I\pmod{61}$, so the
method returns the factor $53$.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Alignment]
Modulo $15$ the frequencies $4$ and $6$ align at $12$: $M_2^{12}\equiv I\pmod{15}$ and
every gcd at $k=12$ equals $15$. This is Theorem~\ref{thm:align} in action, and explains
why the practical algorithm must accumulate the exponent one prime power at a time.
\end{example}

Theorems~\ref{thm:barrier} and \ref{thm:factor} together give the main structural finding:
among the three generators of the Pythagorean tree, exactly one --- the hyperbolic
generator $M_2$ --- carries prime-separating spectral information.

\section{The Berggren--Lucas trace sequence}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{Berggren--Lucas sequence} is
$t(k) = \tr\bigl(M_2^{k}\bigr) = (3+2\sqrt2)^k + (3-2\sqrt2)^k + (-1)^k$, $k\ge0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Cayley--Hamilton and the recurrence]\label{thm:rec}
$M_2^3 = 5M_2^2 + 5M_2 - I$, and consequently
\[
t(k+3) = 5t(k+2) + 5t(k+1) - t(k), \qquad t(0)=3,\ t(1)=5,\ t(2)=35,\ t(3)=197 .
\]
\end{theorem}

The first terms are $3,5,35,197,1155,6725,39203,228485,1331715,7761797,\dots$

\begin{theorem}[Trace splitting]\label{thm:tracesplit}
For an odd prime $p$ and all $k\ge0$, over $\FF_p$,
$\tr\bigl(M_2^{k}\bigr) = \tr\bigl(U^{k}\bigr) + (-1)^k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Berggren--Lucas congruence]\label{thm:lucas}
For every odd prime $p$, $\ t(p)\equiv 5 \pmod p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:frob}, $U^p = 3I + 8^{(p-1)/2}S$ and $S$ is traceless, so
$\tr(U^p)=6$ regardless of the sign. By Theorem~\ref{thm:tracesplit},
$t(p)\equiv 6 + (-1)^p = 5 \pmod p$ since $p$ is odd.
\end{proof}

Note $5 = t(1) = \tr(M_2)$: the Frobenius fixes the trace, as it must, since it permutes
the eigenvalues.

\begin{corollary}[Compositeness witness]
If $n$ is odd and $t(n)\not\equiv 5 \pmod n$, then $n$ is composite.
\end{corollary}

\begin{example}
$t(9) = 7\,761\,797 \equiv 8 \pmod 9$, so $9$ is composite.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Empirical strength]
Of the $122$ odd composites $n$ with $9\le n<400$, the test certifies $118$; the
exceptions --- the first Berggren pseudoprimes --- are $35$, $119$, $169$, $385$.
\end{example}

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Resonance search (Berggren $p\pm1$ method)}

Given $N$ and a smoothness bound $B$, accumulate the exponent $E=\lcmop(1,\dots,B)$ into
$M_2$ modulo $N$ one prime power at a time, taking a gcd after each step:

\begin{verbatim}
Input:  odd composite N, smoothness bound B
Output: a nontrivial factor of N, or FAIL

A <- M2 mod N
for each prime r <= B:
    e <- largest power of r that is <= B
    A <- A^e mod N
    g <- gcd of all entries of (A - I) mod N, together with N
    if 1 < g < N: return g
    if g = N:     return FAIL     # frequencies aligned; back off the exponent
return FAIL
\end{verbatim}

\noindent\textbf{Correctness.} If at some stage the accumulated exponent is a multiple of
$\ord_p(M_2)$ but not of $\ord_q(M_2)$, Theorem~\ref{thm:factor} guarantees $g=p$; if it is
a multiple of both, Theorem~\ref{thm:align} guarantees $g=N$ and the algorithm reports
failure.

\noindent\textbf{Complexity.} $O(\pi(B))$ matrix exponentiations, each $O(\log B)$ modular
$3\times3$ multiplications, so $\tilde O(B)$ modular multiplications of $O(\log N)$-bit
numbers. By Corollary~\ref{cor:order} and Theorem~\ref{thm:hypres} the success condition is
$B$-smoothness of $p-1$ or $p+1$ --- the union of the conditions for Pollard's and
Williams' methods.

\subsection{Exact order computation}

By Theorem~\ref{thm:hypres} the order divides $p\mp1$ according to $p \bmod 8$; factor that
number and remove prime factors greedily from the exponent while the power remains the
identity. By Corollary~\ref{cor:order} the answer equals $\lcmop(2,\ord_p(U))$, so it
suffices to work with the $2\times2$ block. Complexity: one factorisation of $p\mp1$ plus
$O(\log p)$ modular exponentiations.

\subsection{Trace compositeness test}

Compute $t(n)\bmod n$ either by the linear recurrence in $O(n)$ steps or by modular
exponentiation $M_2^n \bmod n$ in $O(\log n)$ matrix multiplications, and compare with $5$.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the resonance mechanism is}

Every result above is a consequence of one picture. The generator $M_2$ acts on a
$3$-dimensional space that splits, over any ring where $2$ is invertible, into a hyperbolic
plane and a reflected line. On the hyperbolic plane the action is multiplication by the
unit $3+2\sqrt2$ of $\ZZ[\sqrt2]$. Reducing mod $p$, that unit lands either in
$\FF_p^\times$ (order $p-1$) or on the norm-one torus of $\FF_{p^2}$ (order $p+1$), and
which is decided by whether $2$ is a square mod $p$, that is, by $p \bmod 8$. The order of
the unit in that group is the resonant frequency, up to the factor of $2$ contributed by
the reflection. Two distinct primes generically place their units in groups of different
orders, so a single exponent can be a multiple of one order and not of the other; the gcd
then separates them.

\subsection{Honest limits}

The factoring criterion is rigorous and unconditional, but its efficiency is exactly that
of the $p\pm1$ family: it is fast only when $p-1$ or $p+1$ is smooth. RSA key generation
relies on the overwhelming probability that random large $p\pm1$ have a large prime factor,
so Theorem~\ref{thm:factor} does not threaten deployed cryptography. Its value is
threefold: it shows that a classical geometric object contains a complete $p\pm1$ factoring
engine with case selection performed by quadratic reciprocity; it gives an exact, not
merely asymptotic, description of the resonance set, making the failure mode explicit; and
it proves a matching negative result about the other two branches.

\subsection{Applications beyond factoring}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Primality testing.} The Berggren--Lucas congruence supplies a Lucas-type test
whose underlying object is a $3$-dimensional representation with a $2$-dimensional torus
part; combining it with a Fermat base-$2$ test is the natural Baillie--PSW analogue.
\item \textbf{Pseudorandomness.} The orbit of $M_2$ on $(\ZZ/p\ZZ)^3$ is confined to the
conic $a^2+b^2=c^2$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:triples}) and has period $p\mp1$, giving a cheap,
structured generator of points on that conic.
\item \textbf{Pythagorean triples mod $p$.} Theorem~\ref{thm:hypres} says the tree of
triples, read modulo $p$, is a finite dynamical system with period dividing $p^2-1$; the
root $(3,4,5)$ returns to itself after $p^2-1$ steps for every odd prime $p$.
\end{itemize}

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Sharpness of the resonance]
For $p\equiv\pm3\pmod8$ write $d(p)=\ord_p(M_2)$. Then $d(p)=p+1$ for a set of primes of
positive natural density, and dually $d(p)=p-1$ for a positive density of
$p\equiv\pm1\pmod8$. Equivalently, $3+2\sqrt2$ is a primitive root of the norm-one torus of
$\FF_p(\sqrt2)$ infinitely often.
\end{conjecture}

The order formula $\ord_p(M_2)=\lcmop(2,\ord_p(U))$ reduces the question to the
multiplicative order of the fixed unit $3+2\sqrt2 = (1+\sqrt2)^2$ in a one-dimensional
torus --- precisely the setting of Artin's primitive-root conjecture for the norm-one
subgroup, where Hooley-type conditional results are available under GRH. The observed
failures at $p=17,29,41,59$ give a ready-made test set.

\begin{conjecture}[No Carmichael numbers for the two-sided test]
There is no odd composite $n$ satisfying simultaneously
\[
t(n)\equiv 5 \pmod n \qquad\text{and}\qquad t(n+1)\equiv t(n-1)\pmod n .
\]
\end{conjecture}

Dropping the second condition, pseudoprimes exist (the smallest being $35$, $119$, $169$,
$385$); the conjecture is that the two-sided test is Carmichael-free, as is believed for the
Baillie--PSW combination. The condition $t(n)\equiv5$ tests the Frobenius on the trace of a
$3$-dimensional representation whose semisimple part is the norm-one torus of
$\ZZ[\sqrt2]$, whereas the second condition tests the torus itself; a counterexample would
have to be simultaneously Fermat-like for the split and the non-split torus at every prime
divisor. The recurrence is linear-time computable, so the conjecture is directly testable
to $10^9$.

\begin{conjecture}[Smoothness dichotomy]
For a semiprime $N=pq$, define $B_{\mathrm{smooth}}(N)$ as the least $B$ such that some
$k\le B!$ is a resonance of $p$ but not of $q$. Then
\[
B_{\mathrm{smooth}}(N) = \min\bigl(B(p-1),B(p+1),B(q-1),B(q+1)\bigr),
\]
where $B(m)$ is the largest prime factor of $m$; i.e.\ the Berggren generator factors $N$ in
time $\tilde O(B_{\mathrm{smooth}}(N))$, matching Pollard $p-1$ and Williams $p+1$
simultaneously with a single fixed matrix.
\end{conjecture}

Further avenues include higher-rank analogues (trees of solutions of other quadratic forms,
each with its own hyperbolic generator and splitting condition, producing a family of
$p\pm1$ engines indexed by real quadratic fields); the distribution of Berggren
pseudoprimes; and the equidistribution of the $M_2$-orbit of $(3,4,5)$ among the points of
the conic $a^2+b^2=c^2$ in $\mathbb{P}^2(\FF_p)$.

\section{Conclusion}

Starting from three integer matrices that generate the tree of primitive Pythagorean
triples, we have determined their exact spectra, identified the hyperbolic eigenvalues as
the square of the silver ratio, and proved that these matrices are Lorentz isometries over
every commutative ring. Reducing modulo a prime, we showed that the two unipotent
generators have order exactly the modulus and provably carry no factoring information,
while the hyperbolic generator has order $\lcmop(2,\ord_p(U))$, dividing $p-1$ or $p+1$
according to $p \bmod 8$ --- a dichotomy that coincides exactly with whether the hyperbolic
eigenvalues are visible in $\FF_p$ or hidden in $\FF_{p^2}$. Misaligned frequencies at the
two primes of a semiprime yield a provably correct factor-extraction criterion, aligned
frequencies provably yield nothing, and the trace sequence of the hyperbolic generator
supplies a Fermat--Lucas compositeness test. The unifying slogan is that the spectrum of an
arithmetic operator is a frequency, and different primes tune it differently; in the
Pythagorean tree, exactly one of the three branches is tuned at all.

\end{document}

